================================================================================
CUDA-Vision: Installation and Environment Setup Guide
Coursera GPU Specialization Capstone Project
Author: Joshua Deniese
================================================================================

This document provides step-by-step instructions for building and running
the CUDA-Vision project across different operating systems and hardware
environments (NVIDIA GPUs, CPU-only development workstations, and Coursera Labs).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Core Prerequisites:
  - C++ Compiler: GCC 9+ (supporting C++17) or Clang 10+
  - Build System: GNU Make (v4.0+) or CMake (v3.18+)
  - Multithreading: OpenMP library (libomp / libgomp)
  - Image/Signal I/O: Self-contained header libraries (stb_image, stb_image_write)
                      included directly in lib/stb/ (NO external OpenCV required).

Optional for GPU Acceleration:
  - NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit 11.0+ or 12.0+ (nvcc compiler and CUDA runtime)
  - NVIDIA Driver: 450.xx or newer
  - CUDA-capable GPU (Compute Capability 5.0+ / Maxwell, Pascal, Volta, Turing, Ampere, Ada, Hopper)

NOTE FOR CPU WORKSTATIONS:
  If an NVIDIA GPU or the CUDA Toolkit is not installed, the project automatically
  builds the optimized Heterogeneous CPU engine with OpenMP multi-threading and
  CUDA grid/block shared-memory emulation. You do NOT need an NVIDIA GPU to build,
  execute, benchmark, and verify this project!

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. LINUX INSTALLATION (Ubuntu / Debian)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1: Install standard C++ build tools and OpenMP
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install -y build-essential cmake libomp-dev

Step 2 (Optional, for NVIDIA GPU hosts): Install NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit
  sudo apt-get install -y nvidia-cuda-toolkit

Step 3: Clone the repository and navigate into the project directory
  git clone <REPOSITORY_URL>
  cd cuda-adv-lib

Step 4: Build the project

  Option A: Auto-detect (automatically chooses GPU if nvcc exists, else CPU):
    make all

  Option B: Force CPU build (optimized OpenMP + CUDA tile emulation):
    make cpu

  Option C: Force GPU build (requires nvcc):
    make gpu

Step 5: Run the automated execution and verification script:
    ./run.sh

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. RUNNING IN COURSERA LABS / GOOGLE COLAB / CLOUD GPU
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

In environments with NVIDIA GPUs (such as Coursera GPU Labs or Google Colab):
  1. Open a terminal session.
  2. Verify your GPU is visible:
       nvidia-smi
       nvcc --version
  3. Clone and build:
       git clone <REPOSITORY_URL>
       cd cuda-adv-lib
       make gpu
  4. Run the full pipeline with GPU acceleration:
       ./bin/cuda_vision --mode gpu --save-all
  5. Run multi-scale scaling benchmarks:
       ./bin/cuda_vision --benchmark

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. LINUX INSTALLATION (Fedora / RHEL / CentOS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  sudo dnf groupinstall -y "Development Tools"
  sudo dnf install -y gcc-c++ make cmake libgomp
  make all

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. MACOS INSTALLATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  brew install libomp cmake
  make cpu
  ./bin/cuda_vision --filter all --save-all

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. VERIFYING THE BUILD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the built-in self-test and verification target:
  make test

This executes the pipeline, verifies algorithm equivalence between the OpenMP
multi-threaded CPU reference and the CUDA shared-memory tiling implementation,
and confirms that the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) reaches 100 dB
(mathematical equivalence).

To clean compiled binaries:
  make clean

To package proof of execution artifacts for submission:
  make package
================================================================================
